# @@NAME@@

A @@DIMENSION@@ game made with [Ferravex](https://ferravex.com/docs/). Steer from the starting
pad to the gold beacon as fast as you can.

## Play

```sh
cargo run                                # in a window
python3 scripts/build-web.py --serve     # in the browser at http://127.0.0.1:8000/
cargo test                               # rules and doc tests, no window needed
```

| Action | Keyboard | Gamepad |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Move | WASD / arrow keys | left stick |
| Start, resume, play again | Enter (or click the button) | A |
| Pause | Escape | Start |
| Restart | R | |
| Rebind controls | F2 | |

Native windows need your platform's display and OpenGL libraries (on Linux:
X11 and GLX). The browser build needs the Wasm target once:
`rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown`.

## Make it yours

| File | What it holds |
| --- | --- |
| `src/game.rs` | The rules: state, fixed-tick `advance`, `restart`, and their tests. Tuning constants sit at the top. |
| `src/lib.rs` | The frame loop (input → rules → drawing) and `draw_world`. `TITLE` is the window title. |
| `src/hud.rs` | Title, timer, key prompts, the main button, the win toast and presentation-only effects. |

A few first edits to try:

- Make the player faster: change `SPEED` in `src/game.rs`.
- Change the look: swap `PostFx::subtle()` for `PostFx::neon()` in `src/lib.rs`,
  or recolor `PLAYER`/`BEACON` and `theme()` in `src/hud.rs`.
- Add a rule: put new state on `Game`, reset it in `Game::restart`, and update
  it inside the tick loop in `Game::advance`. Add a test next to the others.

Three habits keep the game deterministic and smooth:

1. Rules only change inside fixed ticks (`Game::advance`), never while drawing.
2. Drawing reads `Game` and interpolates with the `alpha` that `advance` returns.
3. Effects react to accepted game events (the win), not to raw key presses.

For a new gameplay button (jump, shoot), read it with `PlayerInput::take` or
`take_tick` inside the tick loop so a quick tap is never lost or repeated.
Menu actions (start, pause, restart) stay once per frame, as in `src/lib.rs`.

## Screenshots and smoke runs

```sh
cargo run -- --autoplay --smoke-frames 180 --screenshot shot.png
```

`--autoplay` steers toward the beacon by itself; `--smoke-frames N` exits after N
frames and `--screenshot` saves the last one. In the browser, add `?autoplay`.

## Reference

`Cargo.toml` and `ferravex.json` point at the Ferravex checkout by absolute path;
if the checkout moves, update both. `Cargo.lock` was seeded from it so builds
work offline; commit it with your game. For reproducible builds use
`cargo run --offline --locked`.

This project was generated with
`python3 scripts/new-game.py @@NAME@@ --template @@TEMPLATE@@`. Its generated rate is @@TICK_RATE@@ Hz:
`Game::TICK_RATE_HZ` sets how often the rules run, independently of the frame
rate (at most eight catch-up ticks per frame). `ferravex.json` (schema version 1)
records the generation options (`engine_root`, `package`, `template`,
`tick_rate`, `window_title`) and the generated file list. It is provenance, not
configuration: to retitle the game, edit `TITLE` in `src/lib.rs` and the titles in
`web/index.html` and `web/games.json`.

`scripts/build-web.py` builds the library for `wasm32-unknown-unknown` and stages
the checkout's first-party browser host into `target/web`. `--profile dev` gives a
faster debug build. No npm, wasm-bindgen, registry dependencies or CDN are
involved. Serve over HTTP; opening `index.html` as a file cannot load Wasm.
